{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "11",
    "case_summary": "A 59-year-old woman presented with 2 days of left orbital pain. Medical history was significant for hypertension controlled with lisinopril and several months of epiphora without prior dacryocystitis.\n\nExamination revealed:- Visual acuity: 20/25 OD and 20/400 OS- Edematous, hyperemic left eyelids- Diffuse limitation in extraocular movements in the left eye- Normal intraocular pressure- Normal fundi on both sides\n\n{'Imaging Studies': {'Initial CT with contrast': {'Title': 'Coronal Computed Tomography Scan', 'Description': 'Left-sided lacrimal sac abscess with postseptal fat stranding largely limited to the extraconal space, minimal sinus mucosal thickening, and opacification of both nasolacrimal ducts'}, 'Follow-up CT': {'Title': 'Repeat Contrast-Enhanced Computed Tomography', 'Description': 'Increased abscess size with globe compression. Minimal intraconal fat stranding, and unremarkable optic nerve appearance'}, 'MRI/MRA': {'Title': 'Magnetic Resonance Imaging and Angiography', 'Description': 'Patent ophthalmic artery; diffusion-weighted imaging showed acute injury to the entire orbital segment of the left optic nerve'}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Cultures': 'Initial cultures isolated gram-positive cocci in chains; later identified as pan-susceptible Streptococcus pyogenes'}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Orbital abscess secondary to acute dacryocystitis\nB. Acute Dacryocystitis with Orbital Cellulitis\nC. Orbital cellulitis secondary to dacryocystitis\nD. Acute dacryocystitis with secondary optic nerve injury\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Acute Unilateral Orbital Syndrome with Pain and Ocular Motor Limitation",
      "time_course": "acute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "2 days of left orbital pain",
        "edematous, hyperemic left eyelids",
        "diffuse limitation in extraocular movements in the left eye",
        "left-sided lacrimal sac abscess with postseptal fat stranding",
        "isolated gram-positive cocci in chains; later identified as pan-susceptible Streptococcus pyogenes"
      ],
      "excluded_candidates": [
        "Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma: unlikely due to normal intraocular pressure",
        "Orbital Cellulitis: possible, but lacrimal sac abscess is more specific"
      ],
      "alarm_features": [
        "severe vision loss (20/400 OS)",
        "infection with Streptococcus pyogenes"
      ],
      "salient_findings": [
        "left orbital pain",
        "edematous left eyelid",
        "limited extraocular movements",
        "lacrimal sac abscess",
        "Streptococcus pyogenes infection"
      ]
    },
